Decision Memo

  The current evidence supports a pivot, not more local tuning of the present sequential bridge.

  The reason is structural. The best readout_limited regimes in artifacts/sim/verification-audit/tables/
  regime_classification.csv do show that the current readout layer misses real residual structure: for example
  T=0.25, ratio 16 has mechanism strength 0.968 and oracle gap 0.343. But even there, the oracle benchmark in
  artifacts/sim/verification-audit/tables/readout_sensitivity.csv still leaves very bad bridge-level behavior, with
  aligned same-sign mass 0.400 and max marginal drift 1.979. So better detection helps branch recovery, but it does
  not rescue the sequential mechanism.

  The mechanism-limited rows are more decisive. At T=0.25, ratio 4 and T=1.0, ratio 2, oracle improvement is only
  about 0.08, meaning the practical readouts are already near the oracle ceiling. In one case, T=0.5, ratio 2, the
  practical metric is slightly better than oracle on confidence efficiency. That means the core failure is not
  mainly readout design. It is that the anisotropic-depletion bridge does not reliably produce residual branches
  that also satisfy the needed sequential support and drift constraints.
